From 288e785fca35f3ce111a12e6182a93145d90d700 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Felipe Vasconcelos <Felipe.Vasconcelos@windriver.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 11:54:06 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] Cap federated token lifetime to OIDC IdP token expiry

When Keystone issues a token via the WebSSO/OIDC federation flow,
the token's lifetime is set to the global [token] expiration value
(default 1 hour), regardless of the upstream OIDC IdP's token
lifetime policy. This means an administrator who configures short
OIDC token lifetimes in Dex (e.g., idTokens: 5m) will find that
Horizon sessions persist far beyond that policy.

This patch caps federated token lifetime to the OIDC ID token's
exp claim: min(oidc_exp, global_expiration). This follows the
existing pattern used for application credential expiry capping
(CVE-2022-2447) and ensures federated sessions do not outlive the
IdP's intended session duration.

Changes:
- oauth2_proxy_headers.py: Extract 'exp' claim from JWT and set
  HTTP_OIDC_EXP in the WSGI environ
- token/provider.py: Read HTTP_OIDC_EXP for federated tokens and
  cap expires_at if the OIDC expiry is earlier than the default
- tests: Add test cases for exp extraction and token capping logic

Only federated tokens are affected. Password-authenticated tokens,
service accounts, and CLI tokens remain unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Felipe Vasconcelos <Felipe.Vasconcelos@windriver.com>
